Step 1: Verify the current working directory
pwd

This command displays the current directory path and confirms that you are inside the GitDemo folder.

Step 2: Create a .log file
echo "Error message" > error.log

This command creates a file named error.log and writes the text "Error message" into it.

Step 3: Create a log folder
mkdir log

This command creates a directory named log.

Step 4: Create a file inside the log folder
echo "Test" > log/test.txt

This command creates a file named test.txt inside the log folder.

Step 5: Create a .gitignore file
echo "" > .gitignore

This command creates an empty .gitignore file.

Step 6: Add a rule to ignore all .log files
echo "*.log" >> .gitignore

This command adds the rule *.log to the .gitignore file, which tells Git to ignore all files with the .log extension.

Step 7: Add a rule to ignore the log folder
echo "log/" >> .gitignore

This command adds the rule log/ to the .gitignore file, which tells Git to ignore the entire log directory.

Step 8: Verify the contents of .gitignore
cat .gitignore

This command displays the contents of the .gitignore file.

Expected output:

*.log
log/
Step 9: Check the Git status
git status

This command shows the status of the repository. You will notice that error.log and the log folder are not displayed because they are being ignored by Git.

Step 10: Add .gitignore to the staging area
git add .gitignore

This command stages the .gitignore file for commit.

Step 11: Commit the changes
git commit -m "Added gitignore"

This command creates a commit with the message "Added gitignore".

Step 12: Verify the repository status again
git status
